{"id":"f7ebf0de-a8ad-406b-9771-97bdbe6277b3","object":"chat.completion","created":1785026151,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    nodes = case[\\\"nodes\\\"]\\n    required_symbols = case[\\\"required_symbols\\\"]\\n    node_map = {}\\n    children = {}\\n    for node in nodes:\\n        nid = node[\\\"id\\\"]\\n        node_map[nid] = node\\n        parent = node.get(\\\"parent\\\")\\n        if parent is not None:\\n            children.setdefault(parent, []).append(nid)\\n    retained = set(node_map.keys())\\n    symbol_counts = {}\\n    for node in nodes:\\n        sym = node[\\\"symbol\\\"]\\n        symbol_counts[sym] = symbol_counts.get(sym, 0) + 1\\n    required_set = set(required_symbols)\\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(nodes, key=lambda n: (-n[\\\"depth\\\"], n[\\\"id\\\"]))\\n    removed_order = []\\n    for node in sorted_nodes:\\n        nid = node[\\\"id\\\"]\\n        if not node[\\\"removable\\\"]:\\n            continue\\n        if nid == \\\"root\\\":\\n            continue\\n        if any(c in retained for c in children.get(nid, [])):\\n            continue\\n        if node[\\\"symbol\\\"] in required_set and symbol_counts[node[\\\"symbol\\\"]] <= 1:\\n            continue\\n        retained.remove(nid)\\n        removed_order.append(nid)\\n        symbol_counts[node[\\\"symbol\\\"]] -= 1\\n    retained_ids = sorted(retained)\\n    return {\\n        \\\"removed_node_ids\\\": removed_order,\\n        \\\"retained_node_ids\\\": retained_ids\\n    }\\n\"\n}","reasoning_content":"We need to implement solve(case) for SE-PE-01. Based on the description and fixture payloads, it's about reducing a parse tree by removing nodes that are removable, while preserving required symbols and not removing the root or nodes with retained children. The output should be \"removed_node_ids\" (list of IDs in order of removal) and \"retained_node_ids\" (sorted list of IDs of nodes that remain). \n\nThe case payload has \"nodes\" list with fields: id, parent, symbol, depth, removable, utility. Also \"required_symbols\" list. The root is id \"root\" with parent null and removable false. We need to decide which nodes to remove.\n\nThe description: \"Consider deeper parse-tree nodes before shallower nodes.\" So we should iterate nodes by descending depth? But also \"Use node ID as the stable tie break at equal depth.\" So we can sort nodes by depth descending, then by id ascending.\n\nAlgorithm: Start with all nodes. For each node in that order, check if it's removable, not root, not a required symbol, and that removing it doesn't remove any required symbol (i.e., the symbol is still represented by some other node that remains, or the required symbol might be present in ancestors? Actually required_symbols are symbols that must remain in the tree after reduction. Typically, we need to ensure that after removal, every required symbol still appears in some retained node. Also we cannot remove a node if any of its children are retained (since that would orphan them). So we need to consider tree structure: parents and children.\n\nWe have parent field as string ID. We can build adjacency. Also track which nodes are retained initially. Then for each candidate node, check: if removable==False or id==\"root\" -> skip. Also if any child is retained (i.e., not removed yet) -> skip (cannot remove parent while child retained). Also need to check if removing this node would cause any required_symbol to be missing from the remaining nodes. That is: the node's symbol might be the only occurrence of that required symbol. So we need to count occurrences of each required symbol among retained nodes. When considering removal, we temporarily check if after removal, all required symbols still have at least one node with that symbol among the retained nodes. If yes, then we can remove it.\n\nBut also there is \"utility\" field? The description mentions \"utility\" in fixtures but no mention of using it in the reduction criteria. Possibly it's used as a tie-breaker? But the instruction says \"Use node ID as the stable tie break at equal depth.\" So we ignore utility. Also \"registered_candidate_order\" - maybe it's the order we process nodes: deeper first, then by ID.\n\nWe need to output removed_node_ids in the order they are removed. And retained_node_ids sorted (likely by ID, or by some order? The output schema says \"items type string\", but not explicitly sorted. However the description: \"Return sorted retained IDs\". So sort retained IDs.\n\nLet's implement:\n\n- Build dict node_map: id -> node dict (with all fields).\n- Build children mapping: parent_id -> list of child ids.\n- Initialize retained set with all node ids.\n- Determine required_symbols set.\n- For each required symbol, we need at least one node with that symbol retained.\n\n- Sort nodes by depth descending, then by id ascending (for tie break). Iterate over that sorted list.\n- For each node:\n  - if node[\"removable\"] == False -> skip\n  - if node[\"id\"] == \"root\" -> skip (root removable is false but double-check)\n  - if any child of node is still in retained set -> skip (can't remove parent before children)\n  - if node[\"symbol\"] in required_symbols:\n      - count number of retained nodes with that symbol (including this node). If count == 1, then removing would make it 0, so skip.\n  - If all checks pass:\n      - remove node from retained set\n      - append node[\"id\"] to removed list\n\n- After processing all candidates, retained_node_ids = sorted list of ids in retained set.\n- removed_node_ids = list in order of removal (which we built).\n\nEdge cases: Could there be cycles? No, tree. But ensure we process children before parents. Since we sort by depth descending, we process deeper nodes first, so we remove leaves before parents. That matches \"deeper before shallower\". However note: If a node has children that are also removable, we should remove them first. Our iteration order ensures that. But we also skip if any child retained, so parent will only be considered after all its children are removed. Good.\n\nBut what about nodes that are not removable? They stay.\n\nAlso required_symbols: the root symbol \"root\" is required? It's listed, but root is never removed. That's fine.\n\nWe also need to consider that after removing a node, the required_symbol check for its ancestors might change. Since we process in order, it's fine.\n\nOne more nuance: The node's symbol might be required, but we might have multiple nodes with that symbol. So we need to count all retained nodes with that symbol. We can maintain a count per symbol: initially count from all nodes. When we remove a node, decrement count for its symbol. For each candidate, if its symbol is required, we check if count[symbol] > 1 (or >=2) before removal. If count[symbol] == 1, then removing it would make it 0, so skip.\n\nBut careful: The node might be the only one with that symbol, but it's removable? Then we cannot remove it because required_symbols must remain.\n\nImplementation details:\n\n- nodes list: each node dict has id, parent, symbol, depth, removable (bool), utility (float). But removable may be boolean in Python? The fixture shows true/false which are JSON booleans, so Python will have True/False.\n\n- Build node_map, build children list.\n\n- retained set = set(node_map.keys()).\n\n- symbol_counts = {}\n  for node in nodes: symbol_counts[node[\"symbol\"]] = symbol_counts.get(node[\"symbol\"], 0) + 1\n\n- required_set = set(case[\"required_symbols\"])\n\n- candidate_nodes = sorted(nodes, key=lambda n: (-n[\"depth\"], n[\"id\"]))  # descending depth, ascending id\n\n- removed_order = []\n\n- for node in candidate_nodes:\n    if not node[\"removable\"]: continue\n    if node[\"id\"] == \"root\": continue\n    # check children\n    children = children_map.get(node[\"id\"], [])\n    if any(child_id in retained for child_id in children): continue\n    # check required symbol\n    if node[\"symbol\"] in required_set and symbol_counts[node[\"symbol\"]] <= 1:\n        continue\n    # proceed removal\n    retained.remove(node[\"id\"])\n    removed_order.append(node[\"id\"])\n    symbol_counts[node[\"symbol\"]] -= 1\n\n- After loop, retained_ids = sorted(retained) (alphabetically? IDs are strings, 'root', 'n1', etc. Should use natural sort? Probably just lexicographic. Fixtures show IDs like 'n1','n2',..., 'root'. So sorted() works.\n\n- Output {\"removed_node_ids\": removed_order, \"retained_node_ids\": retained_ids}\n\nBut we need to ensure that we don't remove nodes that are required by being ancestors of required symbols? The description: \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" So we already handle required symbol by checking the condition that after removal, all required symbols still represented. However, what if a node's symbol is not required, but it is an ancestor of a node with required symbol? The requirement is only about symbols, not about nodes. So we can remove ancestors as long as we don't orphan retained children. And we ensure that required symbols remain in the tree, but the nodes with required symbols could be descendants. So we can remove an ancestor if all its children are removed, even if some descendant (not direct child) has a required symbol, as long as that descendant is still retained. But if we remove a node, its descendants that are not direct children? Actually, if we remove a node, all its descendants become orphaned? But we only remove a node if all its direct children have already been removed. So by the time we remove a node, its subtree below the children (grandchildren) are already removed because we process deeper first. So if a required symbol node is a grandchild, its parent (intermediate) must have been removed first, then the grandchild becomes a direct child of the node we are considering? No, let's think: root->n1->n6. n6 is depth 2, n1 depth 1. If n6 is required symbol? Suppose n6 symbol is required. n6 is a child of n1. n6 is depth 2, so processed before n1. n6 is removable? It might be not removable (if its symbol is required, removable might be false? In fixtures, n6 has removable true, but symbol s0 not required. But if required, removable would be false presumably). So n6 stays. n1 is parent of n6, when processing n1, we see it has a child n6 still retained, so we skip n1. So n1 stays. That's correct. So our check \"if any child retained\" prevents removal of ancestors of required symbols that are still present.\n\nBut what about a node that has a required symbol but is removable? That node itself should not be removed because its symbol is required and we check that. So the algorithm handles it.\n\nTest with the first fixture: nodes as given. Let's simulate quickly.\n\nnodes:\nroot depth0 removable false symbol root\nn1 depth1 removable false symbol s1 (required)\nn2 depth1 removable true symbol s2\nn3 depth1 removable false symbol s0\nn4 depth2 removable true symbol s1 (required)\nn5 depth2 removable false symbol s2\nn6 depth2 removable true symbol s0\n\nrequired_symbols: root, s1\n\nCounts: root:1, s1:2 (n1,n4), s2:2 (n2,n5), s0:2 (n3,n6)\n\nSorted by depth desc then id asc:\ndepth2: n4 (id n4), n5 (n5), n6 (n6)\ndepth1: n1, n2, n3\ndepth0: root\n\nProcess:\nn4: removable true, not root. children? n4 has no children (parent is n2, but children list? Let's assume no children. So ok. symbol s1 required, count of s1 is 2 >1, so can remove. Remove n4. retained remove n4, removed_order.append('n4'), s1 count becomes 1.\nn5: removable false -> skip.\nn6: removable true, children? none (parent n1). symbol s0 not required, so ok. Remove n6. retain remove n6, removed_order.append('n6'), s0 count becomes 1.\nNow depth1:\nn1: removable false -> skip.\nn2: removable true, children? n4 was removed, so children list may have n4, but n4 is not in retained, so no retained children. symbol s2 not required, so can remove. Remove n2. retain remove n2, removed_order append 'n2', s2 count becomes 1.\nn3: removable false -> skip.\nroot: removable false -> skip.\n\nRetained: root, n1, n3, n5? Wait n5 was not removable, and retained. So retained set: root, n1, n3, n5. Sorted: ['n1','n3','n5','root']? Actually sorted strings: 'n1','n3','n5','root'. But expected? Let's check if that matches the example? There's no expected output shown, but we can verify logic.\n\nBut note: n5 symbol s2, which is not required. That's fine. However, is there any rule about utility? Not mentioned. So output removed: ['n4','n6','n2']. That seems plausible.\n\nBut the description mentions \"grammar_valid\" and \"interestingness_preserved\". Here grammar_valid might mean that the tree structure after removal must be a valid tree (i.e., no orphaned children). Our algorithm ensures that by only removing nodes without retained children. Interestingness_preserved: \"all required symbols remain represented\". So that's covered.\n\nThus algorithm is likely correct.\n\nNow implement solve(case) accordingly. The case is the inner payload, not the outer fixture row. So function receives a dict with keys \"nodes\", \"required_symbols\", maybe \"_case_nonce\". We ignore nonce.\n\nWe need to return a dict with \"removed_node_ids\" and \"retained_node_ids\".\n\nWrite Python code.\n\nMake sure to use only standard library.\n\nNow produce the required JSON output with \"implementation\" key containing the escaped Python source.\n\nAlso note the instruction: \"Do not access files, the network, environment variables, subprocesses, randomness, or clocks.\" Our code doesn't.\n\nLet's write the code.\n\nPotential edge: When building children map, we might have null parent for root. Use empty string? Parent is null in JSON, becomes None in Python. So we can ignore None.\n\nImplementation:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    nodes = case[\"nodes\"]\n    required_symbols = case[\"required_symbols\"]\n    node_map = {}\n    children = {}\n    for node in nodes:\n        nid = node[\"id\"]\n        node_map[nid] = node\n        parent = node.get(\"parent\")\n        if parent is not None:\n            children.setdefault(parent, []).append(nid)\n    retained = set(node_map.keys())\n    symbol_counts = {}\n    for node in nodes:\n        sym = node[\"symbol\"]\n        symbol_counts[sym] = symbol_counts.get(sym, 0) + 1\n    required_set = set(required_symbols)\n    # sort by depth descending, id ascending\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(nodes, key=lambda n: (-n[\"depth\"], n[\"id\"]))\n    removed_order = []\n    for node in sorted_nodes:\n        nid = node[\"id\"]\n        if not node[\"removable\"]:\n            continue\n        if nid == \"root\":\n            continue\n        # check retained children\n        if any(c in retained for c in children.get(nid, [])):\n            continue\n        # check required symbol\n        if node[\"symbol\"] in required_set and symbol_counts[node[\"symbol\"]] <= 1:\n            continue\n        # remove\n        retained.remove(nid)\n        removed_order.append(nid)\n        symbol_counts[node[\"symbol\"]] -= 1\n    retained_ids = sorted(retained)\n    return {\n        \"removed_node_ids\": removed_order,\n        \"retained_node_ids\": retained_ids\n    }\n\nThat's it.\n\nNow encode as JSON string for the implementation key. Need to escape properly.\n\nLet's produce final answer."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":2142,"completion_tokens":3574,"total_tokens":5716,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":2048},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":3197},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":2048,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":94},"system_fingerprint":"fp_8b330d02d0_prod0820_fp8_kvcache_20260402"}